\title{Universal Approximation with Quantitative Bounds:\\
Explicit Linear and Quadratic Rates for One-Dimensional ReLU Networks}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{June 27, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
The classical universal approximation theorem guarantees that neural networks can
approximate continuous functions arbitrarily well, but it is non-quantitative: it
asserts existence without specifying network size or convergence rate. We give a
fully explicit, constructive, and machine-checked refinement for one-dimensional
single-hidden-layer ReLU networks. Fixing a transparent architecture---the
ramp-difference interpolation network $\reluInterpNet(f,n,\cdot)$ built from $2n$
ReLU neurons that reproduces the uniform piecewise-linear interpolant of $f$ on
$[0,1]$---we prove two sharp regularity-dependent rates. For any $L$-Lipschitz
target the uniform approximation error is at most $L/n$, yielding the width/error
tradeoff $2n = O(1/\varepsilon)$. For targets in the Sobolev class $W^{2,\infty}$
(those with an $M$-Lipschitz derivative) the \emph{same} network achieves the
quadratic rate $M/n^2$, improving the width requirement to
$2n = O(1/\sqrt{\varepsilon})$. The architecture is held fixed throughout; the
exponent on $1/n$ is governed entirely by the smoothness class of the target. The
keystone is an exact algebraic identity showing the network coincides with the
affine interpolant on every grid cell, after which the error analysis reduces to
classical interpolation estimates. All results are formally verified.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

The universal approximation theorem (UAT) states that single-hidden-layer
feedforward networks with a non-polynomial activation are dense in the space of
continuous functions on compact sets. As usually stated it is purely existential:
it does not say \emph{how many neurons} are needed for a target accuracy, nor
\emph{how the error decays} as the network grows. Without such quantitative content
the UAT is a reassurance rather than a design tool.

We supply a quantitative theory in the cleanest setting: scalar functions on
$[0,1]$ approximated by single-hidden-layer ReLU networks. The central object is an
explicit network that exactly reproduces the uniform-grid piecewise-linear
interpolant of the target. Working with this concrete construction rather than an
abstract existence argument lets us track constants exactly.

Our contributions are: (1) an exact architectural identity reducing approximation
to interpolation (Theorem~\ref{thm:cellexact}); (2) a linear rate $L/n$ for
Lipschitz targets (Theorem~\ref{thm:global}) with tradeoff $2n=O(1/\varepsilon)$
(Theorem~\ref{thm:width}); and (3) a quadratic rate $M/n^2$ for $W^{2,\infty}$
targets using the \emph{same} network (Theorem~\ref{thm:sobglobal}) with improved
tradeoff $2n=O(1/\sqrt{\varepsilon})$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:sobwidth}).

\section{Definitions}

Throughout, $n$ is a positive integer and $f : \R \to \R$ is considered on $[0,1]$.

\begin{definition}[ReLU activation]
$\relu(x) = \max(x, 0)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Uniform grid]
The $k$-th node is $\grid(n,k) = k/n$ for $k = 0,\dots,n$. The cell width is
$h = 1/n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Cell slope]
$\cellSlope(f,n,k) = n\bigl(f(\grid(n,k+1)) - f(\grid(n,k))\bigr)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Ramp-difference interpolation network]
\[
\reluInterpNet(f,n,x) = f(0) + \sum_{k=0}^{n-1} \cellSlope(f,n,k)\,
\bigl(\relu(x - \grid(n,k)) - \relu(x - \grid(n,k+1))\bigr).
\]
Each summand uses two ReLU units, giving total hidden width $2n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Lipschitz regularity on $[0,1]$]
$f$ is $L$-Lipschitz on $[0,1]$ if
$|f(x) - f(y)| \le L|x-y|$ for all $x,y \in [0,1]$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Differentiability on $[0,1]$]
$f'$ is a derivative of $f$ on $[0,1]$ if $f$ has derivative $f'(x)$ at every
$x \in [0,1]$. The $W^{2,\infty}$ condition is that such an $f'$ exists and is
itself $M$-Lipschitz.
\end{definition}

\section{The ramp-difference primitive}

\begin{lemma}[Ramp profile]\label{lem:ramp}
For $a \le b$,
\[
\relu(x-a) - \relu(x-b) =
\begin{cases}
0, & x \le a,\\
x - a, & a \le x \le b,\\
b - a, & b \le x.
\end{cases}
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Case-split on the signs of $x-a$ and $x-b$ inside the maxima. For $x \le a$ both
ReLUs vanish; for $a \le x \le b$ the first equals $x-a$ and the second vanishes;
for $b \le x$ both are active and their difference is $b-a$. Each case is a linear
arithmetic check.
\end{proof}

Two grid identities are used repeatedly: $\grid(n,k+1) - \grid(n,k) = 1/n$, and
$\cellSlope(f,n,k)\cdot(\grid(n,k+1)-\grid(n,k)) = f(\grid(n,k+1)) - f(\grid(n,k))$.

\section{The exact identity and the linear rate}

\begin{theorem}[Cellwise exactness]\label{thm:cellexact}
Let $0 < n$, $k < n$, and $x \in [\grid(n,k), \grid(n,k+1)]$. Then
\[
\reluInterpNet(f,n,x) = f(\grid(n,k)) + \cellSlope(f,n,k)\,(x - \grid(n,k)).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Split the defining sum at the active index $k$. For $j < k$, $x$ lies to the right
of cell $j$, so by Lemma~\ref{lem:ramp} each ramp-difference saturates to the cell
width; scaled by the cell slope the term equals $f(\grid(n,j+1)) - f(\grid(n,j))$,
and the sum telescopes to $f(\grid(n,k)) - f(0)$. For $j > k$, $x$ lies left of
cell $j$ and every term vanishes. For $j=k$ the ramp-difference equals
$x - \grid(n,k)$. Adding the base term $f(0)$ cancels the $-f(0)$ from telescoping,
leaving the affine interpolant.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Interpolant error]\label{lem:interp}
If $0 < n$, $k < n$, $0 \le L$, $f$ is $L$-Lipschitz on $[0,1]$, and
$x \in [\grid(n,k), \grid(n,k+1)]$, then
\[
\bigl|\bigl(f(\grid(n,k)) + \cellSlope(f,n,k)(x-\grid(n,k))\bigr) - f(x)\bigr| \le \frac{L}{n}.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
With $t = (x-\grid(n,k))/h \in [0,1]$ and $h = 1/n$, the interpolant is the convex
combination $(1-t)f(\grid(n,k)) + t f(\grid(n,k+1))$. Hence the error is
$(1-t)(f(\grid(n,k)) - f(x)) + t(f(\grid(n,k+1)) - f(x))$. Both nodes lie within
$h = 1/n$ of $x$, so each endpoint deviation is at most $L/n$ by Lipschitzness; a
convex combination of quantities bounded by $L/n$ is again bounded by $L/n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Cellwise linear rate]\label{thm:cellrate}
Under the hypotheses of Lemma~\ref{lem:interp},
$|\reluInterpNet(f,n,x) - f(x)| \le L/n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Substitute Theorem~\ref{thm:cellexact} into Lemma~\ref{lem:interp}.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Cell cover]\label{lem:cover}
If $0 < n$ and $x \in [0,1]$, there is $k < n$ with
$x \in [\grid(n,k), \grid(n,k+1)]$; take $k = \lfloor xn \rfloor$ (and $k=n-1$ at
$x=1$).
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Global linear rate]\label{thm:global}
If $0 < n$, $0 \le L$, and $f$ is $L$-Lipschitz on $[0,1]$, then for all
$x \in [0,1]$,
\[
|\reluInterpNet(f,n,x) - f(x)| \le \frac{L}{n}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Choose the cell of $x$ via Lemma~\ref{lem:cover} and apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:cellrate}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Width/error tradeoff]\label{thm:width}
If $0 < n$, $0 \le L$, $f$ is $L$-Lipschitz on $[0,1]$, and $L \le \varepsilon n$,
then $|\reluInterpNet(f,n,x) - f(x)| \le \varepsilon$ for all $x \in [0,1]$. Hence
hidden width $2n = O(1/\varepsilon)$ suffices for accuracy $\varepsilon$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:global} the error is $\le L/n$, and $L \le \varepsilon n$ gives
$L/n \le \varepsilon$.
\end{proof}

\section{The quadratic rate for Sobolev targets}

\begin{theorem}[Cellwise quadratic rate]\label{thm:sobcell}
Let $0 < n$, $k < n$, $0 \le M$, suppose $f$ has derivative $f'$ on $[0,1]$ and
$f'$ is $M$-Lipschitz on $[0,1]$. Then for
$x \in [\grid(n,k), \grid(n,k+1)]$,
\[
|\reluInterpNet(f,n,x) - f(x)| \le \frac{M}{n^2}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:cellexact} the network equals the affine interpolant
$p(x) = f(a) + S(x-a)$ on $[a,b]$ with $a = \grid(n,k)$, $b = \grid(n,k+1)$,
$h = b-a = 1/n$, and chord slope $S = (f(b) - f(a))/h$. Let $e = f - p$; then
$e(a) = 0$ and $e'(x) = f'(x) - S$. By the mean value theorem there is
$c \in (a,b)$ with $S = f'(c)$, so
\[
|e'(x)| = |f'(x) - f'(c)| \le M|x-c| \le Mh.
\]
Thus $e$ is $(Mh)$-Lipschitz with $e(a)=0$, giving
$|e(x)| = |e(x) - e(a)| \le Mh|x-a| \le Mh^2 = M/n^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Global quadratic rate]\label{thm:sobglobal}
Under the same hypotheses, $|\reluInterpNet(f,n,x) - f(x)| \le M/n^2$ for all
$x \in [0,1]$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Cover $[0,1]$ by cells (Lemma~\ref{lem:cover}) and apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:sobcell}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Improved width/error tradeoff]\label{thm:sobwidth}
For a $W^{2,\infty}$ target with bound $M$, accuracy $\varepsilon$ is reached when
$n \ge \sqrt{M/\varepsilon}$, i.e. hidden width $2n = O(1/\sqrt{\varepsilon})$---the
square root of the Lipschitz requirement.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:sobglobal} the error is $\le M/n^2 \le \varepsilon$ whenever
$n^2 \ge M/\varepsilon$.
\end{proof}

\section{Discussion}

Theorems~\ref{thm:global} and~\ref{thm:sobglobal} share an identical network---one
hidden layer of $2n$ ReLU ramps---yet deliver $O(1/n)$ and $O(1/n^2)$ error. The
only difference is the smoothness class of the target. Thus, with the architecture
fixed, the \emph{exponent} in the convergence rate is determined by the regularity
of the target, not by the network. This converts the qualitative UAT into an
engineering contract: given a regularity certificate, one reads off the neuron
budget for any prescribed accuracy.

\begin{remark}
The quadratic theorem never assumes a second derivative \emph{exists}; a Lipschitz
first derivative ($W^{2,\infty}$) is the exact and strictly weaker hypothesis, and
is precisely what the interpolation-remainder argument requires.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
The quadratic bound is stated with constant $M$ (giving $Mh^2$), not the
textbook-sharp $M/8$ (giving $Mh^2/8$), which is attained by $f(x)=x^2$ at cell
midpoints. Sharpening the constant is left for future work.
\end{remark}

\section{Future directions}

\textbf{C1. Sharp constant $M/(8n^2)$.} The cellwise error equals the interpolation
remainder $\tfrac12 f''(\xi)(x-a)(x-b)$, maximized at the cell midpoint where
$|(x-a)(x-b)| = h^2/4$, yielding $Mh^2/8$.

\textbf{C2. Higher-order rates need depth.} A shallow ReLU network of width $w$
should not approximate every $f \in W^{s,\infty}$ ($s \ge 3$) better than $w^{-2}$,
whereas depth $O(\log(1/\varepsilon))$ achieves $w^{-s}$; depth is required to
compose the squaring map.

\textbf{C3. Total variation as depth-separation currency.} The minimal $L^1$ weight
mass of a shallow approximant of $g$ should be $\Theta(\mathrm{TV}(g) - O(\varepsilon))$,
making total variation the governing invariant.

\textbf{C4. Multidimensional tent.} Extend the tensor-product construction to $d$
dimensions to quantify the depth advantage against input dimension.

\section{Conclusion}

By committing to one transparent architecture---the $2n$-neuron ramp-difference
network reproducing the uniform piecewise-linear interpolant---we converted the
existential universal approximation theorem into explicit quantitative law: error
$L/n$ for Lipschitz targets and $M/n^2$ for $W^{2,\infty}$ targets, with
$O(1/\varepsilon)$ and $O(1/\sqrt{\varepsilon})$ width budgets respectively. The
exponent on $1/n$ is a property of the target's regularity, not of the network.

